Q: agora-rtc-react Screen Sharing to Audience A: <p>I've ran into the same issue for the basic needs i solved it with a new custom hook</p>
<pre><code>import type { IAgoraRTCClient, ILocalVideoTrack } from &quot;agora-rtc-react&quot;;
import AgoraRTC from &quot;agora-rtc-react&quot;;
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from &quot;react&quot;;

interface Props {
  userUid: number; // current user's UID
  client: IAgoraRTCClient; // Screenshare Agora RTC client instance
  appId: string; // Agora App ID
  channel: string; // Agora channel name
  token: string | null; // Agora token
  defaultScreenShareOn?: boolean; // Optional: default state of screen sharing
}

export function useRTCScreenShare({ userUid, client, appId, channel, token, defaultScreenShareOn = false }: Props) {
  const [screenShareOn, setScreenShareOn] = useState(defaultScreenShareOn);
  const screenTrack = useRef&lt;ILocalVideoTrack | null&gt;(null);

  useEffect(() =&gt; {
    if (screenShareOn) {
      // handle sharing
      const handleScreenshareJoin = async () =&gt; {
        // screenshare without audio
        const screenShareTrack = await AgoraRTC.createScreenVideoTrack(
          {
            optimizationMode: &quot;detail&quot;,
          },
          &quot;disable&quot;,
        );
        screenTrack.current = screenShareTrack;
        // join with the screenshare client using a different uid, userUid - 1 is an example
        await client.join(appId, channel, token ? token : null, userUid - 1);
        // publish the screen track
        await client.publish(screenShareTrack);
      };
      handleScreenshareJoin();
    } else {
      // handle leaving
      const handleScreenshareLeave = async () =&gt; {
        if (screenTrack.current) {
          // unpublish and close the screen track
          await client.unpublish(screenTrack.current);
          screenTrack.current.close();
          screenTrack.current = null;
        }
        // leave with the screenshare client
        await client.leave();
      };
      handleScreenshareLeave();
    }
    // Cleanup on unmount
    return () =&gt; {
      if (screenTrack.current) {
        client.unpublish(screenTrack.current);
        screenTrack.current.close();
        screenTrack.current = null;
      }
      client.leave();
    };
  }, [screenShareOn]);

  return {
    screenShareOn,
    setScreenShareOn,
    screenTrack: screenTrack.current,
  };
}
</code></pre>
<p>this can handle the screenshare from joining a new client, showing the streamed video, and leaving, only that i disabled the audio in this case, and for the rendering part it looks something like this:</p>
<pre><code>...
const screenShareClient = AgoraRTC.createClient({ mode: &quot;rtc&quot;, codec: &quot;vp8&quot; });

const { screenShareOn, setScreenShareOn, screenTrack } = useRTCScreenShare({
    userUid: profile.id, // your current users id to use
    client: screenShareClient,
    appId,
    channel,
    token,
});
...
return (&lt;&gt;
    ...
    &lt;AgoraRTCScreenShareProvider client={screenShareClient}&gt;
        {screenTrack &amp;&amp; (
            &lt;LocalVideoTrack
                disabled={!screenShareOn}
                play={screenShareOn}
                style={{ width: &quot;100%&quot;, height: 300 }}
                track={screenTrack}
            /&gt;
        )}
    &lt;/AgoraRTCScreenShareProvider&gt;
    {remoteUsers
        .filter((user) =&gt; user.uid !== profile.id - 1)
        .map((user) =&gt; (
        &lt;div key={user.uid}&gt;
            &lt;RemoteUser user={user} style={{ width: &quot;100%&quot;, height: 300 }}&gt;
            &lt;samp&gt;{user.uid}&lt;/samp&gt;
            &lt;/RemoteUser&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
    ))}
    ...
&lt;/&gt;)
...
</code></pre>
<p>This basically means that we create a client for the screenshare, which we add to the other provider (this can be nested inside the normal <code>AgoraRTCProvider</code>) and use the hook we created, when we turn on the screenshare, it will create a new <code>screenTrack refobject</code>, and show the video for the user, and if we gave the <code>uid</code> correctly (in the example its using the <code>profile.id - 1</code>), then we can filter down the <code>remoteUsers</code> as the new connection from the <code>screenShareClient</code> counts as a new user.</p>
<p>For this example i was using a modified React JS example from the Agora documentation:<br />
<a href="https://docs.agora.io/en/video-calling/get-started/get-started-sdk?platform=react-js" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://docs.agora.io/en/video-calling/get-started/get-started-sdk?platform=react-js</a></p>
<p>some of the previous commenters information from the github page they linked:<br />
<a href="https://github.com/AgoraIO-Extensions/agora-rtc-react/blob/main/packages/agora-rtc-react/docs/hooks/useLocalScreenTrack.en-US.mdx" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/AgoraIO-Extensions/agora-rtc-react/blob/main/packages/agora-rtc-react/docs/hooks/useLocalScreenTrack.en-US.mdx</a></p>
<p>and also the web documentation of screensharing from Agora:<br />
<a href="https://docs.agora.io/en/video-calling/advanced-features/screen-sharing?platform=web#share-screen-and-start-video-simultaneously" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://docs.agora.io/en/video-calling/advanced-features/screen-sharing?platform=web#share-screen-and-start-video-simultaneously</a></p>

Q: Creating a ServiceBase class for client apps A: <p>Sounds like when you are starting the APIService using the main thread. Have you tried to thread off this part so that the service does not hold onto the calling thread?  When the calling thread is tied up the Client App will not be responsive.. not sure this fits, it's a guess. but I have experienced this issue when attempting to start an inproc service.</p>
<pre><code>Task.Run(  ()=&gt;{ builder.Services.AddSingleton&lt;IApiService, new ApiService&gt;();});
</code></pre>

Q: CTAD in a variadic template function A: <p>Introducing template function which is able to print (log) single item, can be very helpful to make this more universal and more flexible:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;iomanip&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;initializer_list&gt;
#include &lt;ranges&gt;
#include &lt;source_location&gt;
#include &lt;type_traits&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;
#include &lt;magic_enum/magic_enum.hpp&gt;
#include &lt;magic_enum/magic_enum_iostream.hpp&gt;

using namespace magic_enum::ostream_operators;

enum class Bar {
    bar1,
    bar2,
};

template &lt;typename T&gt;
concept is_printable = requires(T x, std::ostream&amp; out) {
    { out &lt;&lt; x } -&gt; std::same_as&lt;std::ostream&amp;&gt;;
};

template &lt;typename R&gt;
concept range_to_print = !is_printable&lt;R&gt; &amp;&amp; std::ranges::range&lt;R&gt; &amp;&amp;
                         is_printable&lt;std::ranges::range_reference_t&lt;R&gt;&gt;;

void log_print(const char* s) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot; &quot; &lt;&lt; std::quoted(s);
}

void log_print(const std::string&amp; s) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot; &quot; &lt;&lt; std::quoted(s);
}

void log_print(std::string&amp;&amp; s) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot; &quot; &lt;&lt; std::quoted(s);
}

void log_print(std::string_view s) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot; &quot; &lt;&lt; std::quoted(s);
}

void log_print(char ch) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot; '&quot; &lt;&lt; ch &lt;&lt; '\'';
}

template &lt;is_printable T&gt;
void log_print(T&amp;&amp; x) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot; &quot; &lt;&lt; x;
}

template &lt;range_to_print T&gt;
void log_print(T&amp;&amp; r) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot; [&quot;;
    for (auto&amp; x : r) {
        log_print(x);
        std::cout &lt;&lt; ',';
    }
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot; ]&quot;;
}

template &lt;typename... Ts&gt;
struct debug {
    debug(Ts&amp;&amp;... ts,
          const std::source_location&amp; loc = std::source_location::current()) {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; loc.function_name() &lt;&lt; &quot;:&quot; &lt;&lt; loc.line();
        (log_print(std::forward&lt;Ts&gt;(ts)), ...);
        std::cout &lt;&lt; std::endl;
    }
};

template &lt;typename... Ts&gt;
debug(Ts&amp;&amp;...) -&gt; debug&lt;Ts...&gt;;

template &lt;typename T, typename... Ts&gt;
debug(std::initializer_list&lt;T&gt;, Ts&amp;&amp;...) -&gt; debug&lt;std::initializer_list&lt;T&gt;, Ts...&gt;;

int main() {
    // Option 1: using one enum from the enum class
    debug(Bar::bar1, 5, 'A', 3.14f, &quot;foo&quot;);
    // Option 2: using a vector with items from an enum class
    debug({Bar::bar1, Bar::bar2}, 5, 'A', 3.14f, &quot;foo&quot;);
    // Option 3: without providing any item from the enum class
    debug(&quot;bar&quot;, 123, 2.72, std::string{&quot;string&quot;});
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/sG16x5WK7" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/sG16x5WK7</a></p>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>int main():80 bar1 5 'A' 3.14 &quot;foo&quot;
int main():82 [ bar1, bar2, ] 5 'A' 3.14 &quot;foo&quot;
int main():84 &quot;bar&quot; 123 2.72 &quot;string&quot;
</code></pre>

Q: Shall I use macros or variadic templates to manage output? A: <p>I'd use a log streamer:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;

struct ErrorLogLine {
  std::ostream* os = nullptr;

  ErrorLogLine() = default;
  ErrorLogLine(ErrorLogLine&amp;&amp; other):os(other.os) { other.os = nullptr; }

  ErrorLogLine(std::string msg, std::ostream&amp; s):os(&amp;s) { if (os) *os &lt;&lt; &quot;[&quot; &lt;&lt; msg &lt;&lt; &quot;]: &quot;; }
  ~ErrorLogLine() { if(os) *os &lt;&lt; std::endl; }

  template&lt;class T&gt;
  friend ErrorLogLine&amp;&amp; operator&lt;&lt;( ErrorLogLine&amp;&amp; self, T const&amp; t ) {
    if (self.os) *self.os &lt;&lt; t;
    return std::move(self);
  }

  // std::endl support
  friend ErrorLogLine&amp;&amp; operator&lt;&lt;( ErrorLogLine&amp;&amp; self, std::ostream&amp;(*manip)(std::ostream&amp;) ) {
    if (self.os &amp;&amp; manip) *self.os &lt;&lt; manip;
    return std::move(self);
  }
};
</code></pre>
<p>now we can do:</p>
<pre><code>ErrorLogLine error() { return {&quot;ERROR&quot;, std::cerr}; }

ErrorLogLine info() { return {&quot;INFO&quot;, std::cout}; }
</code></pre>
<p>and use them like:</p>
<pre><code>error() &lt;&lt; &quot;This is the wrong chicken&quot;;
info() &lt;&lt; &quot;The chicken has crossed the road&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/YoqcnrxaT" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live example</a>.</p>
<p>No macros, no variadics.  Output is:</p>
<pre><code>[ERROR]: This is the wrong chicken
[INFO]: The chicken has crossed the road
</code></pre>
<p>where the first one is to <code>cerr</code> and the second to <code>cout</code>, and each line has a io flush at the end.</p>
<p>You can also add <code>error_if(bool)</code> that conditionally prints.  Here a macro might be a good idea, so on failure you can include the text of the code that failed the test.</p>
<pre><code>ErrorLogLine error_if(bool b){ if (b) return error(); else return {}; }
</code></pre>
<p>Each <code>ErrorLogLine</code> can only be used while temporary (or manually <code>std::move</code>'d), and when it is finally destroyed terminates the line with a <code>std::endl</code> to flush the io buffer.</p>
<p>I might make that flush optional, as it can get expensive, but that is what your original design did.</p>

Q: How can I call a method that consumes &#39;self&#39; on a boxed trait object? A: <p>Applying <code>commit</code> to the boxed object is not allowed, because the trait object doesn't know its size (and it's not constant at compile-time). Since you plan to use listeners as boxed objects, you can acknowledge that <code>commit</code> will be invoked on the box and change its signature accordingly:</p>
<pre><code>trait Listener {
    fn some_action(&amp;mut self);
    fn commit(self: Box&lt;Self&gt;);
}

struct FooListener {}

impl Listener for FooListener {
    fn some_action(&amp;mut self) {
        println!(&quot;{:?}&quot;, &quot;Action!!&quot;);
    }

    fn commit(self: Box&lt;Self&gt;) {
        println!(&quot;{:?}&quot;, &quot;Commit&quot;);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This enables <code>Transaction</code> to compile as you wrote it, because inside the implementation of <code>FooListener</code> the size of <code>Self</code> is well-known, and it is perfectly possible to move the object out of the box and consume both.</p>
<p>The price of this solution is that <code>Listener::commit</code> now <em>requires</em> a <code>Box</code>. If that is not acceptable, you could declare both <code>commit(self)</code> and <code>commit_boxed(self: Box&lt;Self&gt;)</code> in the trait, requiring all types to implement both, possibly using private functions or macros to avoid code duplication. This is not very elegant, but it would satisfy both the boxed and unboxed use case without loss of performance.</p>
